Integrate the acceleration:
$\begin{align*}\underset{\sim}{a}&=-10\underset{\sim}{j}\\\underset{\sim}{v}&=C_{1}\underset{\sim}{i}+(-10t+C_{2})\underset{\sim}{j}.\end{align*}$

Using the initial velocity gives $C_{1}=27$ and $C_{2}=36$, so
$\underset{\sim}{v}(t)=27\underset{\sim}{i}+(36-10t)\underset{\sim}{j}\text{ m/s}$.

Integrate again:
$\underset{\sim}{r}=(27t+C_{3})\underset{\sim}{i}+(36t-5t^{2}+C_{4})\underset{\sim}{j}$.

Using $\underset{\sim}{r}(0)=18\underset{\sim}{j}$ gives $C_{3}=0$ and $C_{4}=18$. Hence,
$\underset{\sim}{r}(t)=27t\underset{\sim}{i}+(36t-5t^{2}+18)\underset{\sim}{j}\text{ m}$.
